WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.840
Good validation begins with clear expectations.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.220
Before writing validation logic, define what acceptable input looks like.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:16.720
That definition should include data type, length, format, allowed range, required and optional fields, allowed values, and relationships between fields.

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:23.980
Without a clear contract, validation often becomes a patchwork of checks that are hard to maintain and easy to bypass accidentally.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.360
Type and format are only the beginning.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.660
A date may be syntactically valid but outside the allowed business window.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.280
A numeric value may parse correctly but exceed a safe or sensible range.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.540
A string may match a pattern but refer to a value the current user is not allowed to select.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:44.600
Real validation includes both technical shape and business meaning.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.060
Relationships between fields matter.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.000
A start date should usually come before an end date.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.180
A shipping method may depend on destination country.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.920
A discount code may apply only to certain product categories.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.780
An account identifier may need to match the authenticated user or tenant context.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.200
These checks are validation and authorization-adjacent business rules, and they should be explicit.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.860
Clear contracts make validation easier to review and test.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:20.520
Schemas, request models, typed objects, and documented API contracts help developers, testers, and security reviewers understand what the system expects.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:29.060
When the contract is visible, invalid input becomes easier to reject consistently, and future changes are less likely to create ambiguous behavior.
